  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe mid-level shortwave trough continues to push northeast out of\neastern Montana towards southern Saskatchewan and northwestern \nNorth Dakota. As dynamic lift leaves the region, shower and \nembedded thunderstorm activity will diminish from southwest to \nnortheast through the remainder of the afternoon into the evening.\n\n\nBehind the departing wave an upper level ridge of high pressure\nbuilds over the Northern Plains through Thursday. This will\nestablish a warming and drying trend across eastern Montana. \nThursday will be the warmest day of the with with high \ntemperatures climbing into the upper 70s and 80s which is roughly\n15 degrees above normal for late September.\n\nThe ridge shifts eastward into the Dakotas on Friday as a trough\nmoves into the Pacific Northwest. A deepening surface low over\ncentral Montana will cause a tight pressure gradient producing\nstrong southerly winds across eastern Montana. These winds may\napproach Lake Wind Advisory criteria for Fort Peck Lake. \n\nA cold front is expected to sweep across the forecast area late\nFriday night into early Saturday morning. Wind directions will\nshift sharply to the west-northwest behind the front. The winds\nwill remain elevated throughout Saturday, likely prolonging the\npotential for hazardous conditions on area lakes. Moisture\nassociated with front will be fairly limited, so no precipitation\nis expected with this frontal passage. \n\nWesterly upper level flow persist across the region through much\nof next week. Guidance has begun to show a disturbance moving\nacross the region on Monday. This feature represents the next\npotential for widespread rain across the area.",
